  "response": "The latency collapse is caused by blocking synchronous I/O inside `async` route handlers. These calls freeze the event loop, creating head-of-line blocking across otherwise unrelated requests. CPU remains low because the process is waiting, not computing.\n\nRanked by likely contribution:\n\n1. `requests.get()` — `api/routes.py:21`, `get_invoice`  \n   Synchronous network latency is variable and potentially long. Every downstream slowdown blocks the event loop and amplifies directly into service-wide p99 latency.\n\n2. `subprocess.run()` — `api/routes.py:31`, `export_invoice`  \n   Blocks until the child process exits. It may be the worst call per invocation, although its total production impact depends on export traffic.\n\n3. `time.sleep()` — `api/routes.py:32`, `export_invoice`  \n   A guaranteed event-loop stall. Concurrent requests cannot progress for the full sleep duration.\n\n4. `open()` — `api/routes.py:23`, `get_invoice`  \n   Usually shorter than the others, but still synchronous filesystem I/O on the event loop. Cold storage or a network-mounted filesystem could make it significant.\n\nAt finding level, `crime_00003` (`get_invoice`) is probably the largest aggregate contributor if it is the commonly used read endpoint. `crime_00004` (`export_invoice`) likely causes the largest stall per request. Traffic and timing data would be needed to order those two conclusively.\n\nFix the network call with an async client such as `httpx.AsyncClient`, replace `time.sleep()` with `await asyncio.sleep()`, use async subprocess APIs, and move file access off the event loop or preload/cache it. The datetime, import-cycle, large-function, and deep-import findings do not explain this load-dependent latency pattern.",
